Most SME marketing plans are either missing entirely or buried in a 40-slide deck that nobody reads after the board meeting. Neither works.

A good marketing plan is a working document — short, specific, and built around your actual sales targets.

## Why Most SME Marketing Plans Fail

**Too broad.** "Increase brand awareness" is not a marketing objective. It's a wish.

**No connection to revenue.** Marketing plans that aren't connected to sales targets and pipeline goals float free of any accountability.

**Not updated.** A plan written in January that's never reviewed in March, June, or September is a historical document, not a guide.

## The One-Page Framework That Works

A practical B2B marketing plan for an SME needs six things:

### 1. Business Goal (the anchor)
What is the business trying to achieve in the next 12 months? Revenue target, new market, specific product launch?

Everything in the marketing plan flows from this.

### 2. Target Segments
Who are you selling to? Job title, company size, industry, geography, pain point. The more specific, the better.

### 3. Key Messages
What do you say to each segment? What problem do you solve? What makes you different from the alternatives?

### 4. Channel Mix
Where will you reach each segment? Two or three channels maximum for most SMEs.

### 5. Budget Allocation
How much per channel, per quarter? Include production costs, not just media spend.

### 6. KPIs and Review Cadence
What do you measure weekly? Monthly? What triggers a strategy change?

## The Free Template

The plan itself fits on one page, with a box for each part of the framework above. Three pages of worksheets sit behind it, and the download comes as a PDF or a Word file.

![Page 1 of the free B2B marketing plan template: six boxes for business goal, target segments, key messages, channel mix, budget, and KPIs and review](/images/journal/b2b-marketing-plan-template-preview.webp)

It includes:

- Business goal and marketing OKR format
- Segment definition worksheet
- Messaging canvas per segment
- Channel selection guide with budget benchmarks
- KPI tracking table
- Quarterly review checklist

## How to Use It

1. Fill in the business goal section with your revenue target for the next 12 months
2. Define no more than two primary segments — the buyers you most want and can reach
3. Write three messages per segment: the problem, your solution, your proof
4. Choose two channels and allocate budget against each
5. Set a monthly 30-minute review in your calendar

The plan should fit on one page. If it doesn't, you're trying to do too much.

## A Worked Example

The plan below is filled in for a hypothetical Scottish engineering firm selling to manufacturers. The budget figures come from the ranges in my [guide to setting up B2B marketing campaigns](/journal/practical-guide-to-setting-up-marketing-campaigns-for-b2b-smes/).

| Box | Example entry |
|---|---|
| 1. Business goal | 20 qualified sales enquiries from Scottish manufacturing companies in Q3 2026 |
| 2. Target segments | Operations directors at manufacturers with 50 to 250 staff, triggered by a failed audit or a new production line |
| 3. Key messages | Problem: unplanned downtime. Solution: a maintenance plan built around the line. Proof: a named client and the hours they saved |
| 4. Channel mix | Google Search on service terms, LinkedIn ads to operations job titles |
| 5. Budget | £1,000 a month on Google, £1,500 on LinkedIn, plus production time for one case study |
| 6. KPIs and review | Enquiries and cost per enquiry, checked monthly. If a channel brings no enquiries for two months, move its budget |

## Using It for a Content Marketing Plan

The same six boxes work when content is the main channel. Box 3 becomes the three or four topics each segment searches for, with one piece of proof per topic. Box 4 lists where the content lives and how it travels: your site, email, LinkedIn. Box 5 counts writing and design hours as well as any paid distribution. In box 6, count the enquiries your content brings in; page views can't show whether the plan pays.

## Using It for a B2B SaaS Company

For software, write box 1 as new annual recurring revenue or a number of paying customers, then work back to trials or demos booked. Segment by company size and the moment that starts a search, such as a new hire or an expiring contract with a rival tool. Box 6 tracks trial sign-ups, activation and demos, because a SaaS plan that stops at leads can't show you where trials fail to convert.

## Getting the Most from It

The template is a starting point. The real value comes from the discipline of reviewing it every month and asking: what's working, what's not, and what do we change next quarter?

